ent polling lead averaging around 7 points, including recent October surveys", "Biggs' polarizing conservative brand and lingering controversy over his Colorado City account", "Ratings consensus of Lean D with prediction markets pricing Hobbs near 87%"], "rationale": "Polls since August consistently show Hobbs ahead by margins from 5 to 15 points, and she holds a large financial edge as an incumbent in a slightly Republican-leaning state. Biggs' hard-right profile and recent negative headlines give little evidence of a closing race, though the polling is sparse enough to keep uncertainty wide.", "watch": "A late shift in the race environment or a consolidated anti-Hobbs vote around Biggs could narrow the margin; the October debate Hobbs skipped is worth monitoring for any traction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against Republican Congressman Andy Biggs, with third-party candidates also on the ballot. Hobbs has raised roughly $10 million to Biggs' $3.2 million and enters October with a steady polling lead, including a recent survey showing her up five among likely voters. Biggs faces questions about his shifting account of a Colorado City bill, and split opinion among Mormon women could complicate his coalition.\n\nMost race raters call the contest Lean Democratic, with one rating it Likely Democratic, and prediction markets put Hobbs' chances in the high-80s. Biggs has campaigned on rolling back Hobbs' vetoes, but the race has remained stable in her favor through the fall.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9047, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9201, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9198, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8839, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8882, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem